<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Huan of Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">132–168</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Huan of Han was emperor of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> from 146 to 168. In 159 he used the palace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> to destroy the powerful consort clan of Liang Ji, after which eunuchs dominated the court, and in 166 he began the Partisan Prohibitions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a> who opposed them. His reign saw frequent raids by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> under Tanshihuai.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emperor Huan, born Liu Zhi in 132, was the son of Liu Yi, the Marquess of Liwu, and his concubine Yan Ming, and a great-grandson of Emperor Zhang. In July 146 the powerful official Liang Ji poisoned the young Emperor Zhi, who had called him an arrogant general. Many officials favoured Emperor Zhi's cousin Liu Suan, the Prince of Qinghe, but Liang Ji insisted on Liu Zhi, who was betrothed to his sister Liang Nüying and relatively young, so that he could be controlled. Liu Zhi took the throne on 1 August 146, at the age of 14.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Empress Dowager Liang remained regent, though her brother Liang Ji held more and more control. In 147 Emperor Huan married Liang Nüying, making her empress. In that year Liang Ji, with two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and with the emperor's clear approval, falsely accused the honest officials Li Gu and Du Qiao of plotting to replace him with Liu Suan; both were executed, and Liu Suan took his own life after being demoted. After the empress dowager died in 150, Liang Ji's violence and corruption grew.</p><p>In 159, after Empress Liang died, Emperor Huan learned that Liang Ji had sent assassins against the mother of a consort, and he conspired with five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> to overthrow him. The imperial guards surrounded Liang Ji's house, and Liang Ji committed suicide. The Liang and Sun clans were arrested and slaughtered, and the confiscated property allowed taxes to be reduced by half for a year.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> who had helped were rewarded with marquessates and posts of great power, and they became corrupt with the emperor's approval. In 159 he had the county magistrate Li Yun executed for a petition urging him to curb them. In 161 he offered minor offices for sale to pay for campaigns against Qiang rebellions. In 166 the governor Li Ying was arrested, and more than 200 university students who petitioned for his release were arrested as well; this was later called the first Disaster of Partisan Prohibition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Hanshu</a> records that a Roman envoy reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 166 and was greeted by the emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Emperor Huan died in January 168 without a son, aged 36, after reigning more than 21 years. Empress Dou chose the 11-year-old Liu Hong, who became Emperor Ling. Huan has largely been viewed as an emperor who might have had some intelligence but lacked wisdom, and his reign is held to have contributed greatly to the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a>. The cycle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>' rise and fall continued until the reign of Emperor Ling, when an army led by the regent He Jin and the noblemen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a> massacred the eunuchs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Huan of Han” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
